            <ArticleTitle>Cervical Spondylitis and Epidural Abscess Caused by Brucellosis: a Case Report and Literature Review.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>289-292</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1515/folmed-2016-0035</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">/j/folmed.2016.58.issue-4/folmed-2016-0035/folmed-2016-0035.xml</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Brucellosis is a zoonotic disease widely seen in endemic regions and that can lead to systemic involvement. The musculoskeletal system is frequently affected, and the disease can exhibit clinical involvements such as arthritis, spondylitis, spondylodiscitis, osteomyelitis, tenosynovitis and bursitis. Spondylitis and spondylodiscitis, common complications of brucellosis, predominantly affect the lumbar and thoracic vertebrae. Epidural abscess may occur as a rare complication of spondylitis. Spinal brucellosis and development of epidural abscess in the cervical region are rare. Development of epidural abscess affects the duration and success of treatment. Spinal brucellosis should be considered in patients presenting with fever and lower back-neck pain in endemic regions, and treatment must be initiated with early diagnosis in order to prevent potential complications.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
